import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var r={ohmsLaw:{theory:{concept:"[[Ohm's law for a section of a circuit: the current in a conductor is directly proportional to the voltage across it and inversely proportional to its resistance, I = U / R.]] Voltage is what drives the charges around a circuit; resistance shows how strongly a conductor hinders their motion and is measured in ohms (1 \u03A9 = 1 V/A). Georg Ohm found the law in 1827. It holds for metal conductors at constant temperature, but not for every device \u2014 a diode, for example, does not obey it."},section:{water:{title:"A picture with water",text:"Think of a water pipe. The current I is how much water flows each second. The voltage U is the pressure that pushes the water: the pump (the battery) makes it. The resistance R is how narrow, long or clogged the pipe is. More pressure gives more flow; a narrower pipe gives less flow. The picture is only an analogy: the real thing is electrons, which are pushed by the electric field in the wire."},ohm:{title:"Ohm's law",text:"[[Double the voltage and the current doubles; double the resistance and the current halves.]] The same rule gives R = U/I (how to measure a resistance with a voltmeter and an ammeter) and U = I\xB7R. On the I\u2013U graph a resistor gives a straight line through the origin; the steeper the line, the smaller the resistance."},resistance:{title:"What decides the resistance of a wire?",text:"The material, the length and the thickness: R = \u03C1\xB7L/A. A longer wire resists more (the charges must go farther), a thicker wire resists less (there is more room to pass). The resistivity \u03C1 is a property of the material: copper is among the best conductors (1.7\xB710\u207B\u2078 \u03A9\xB7m), so it is used for cables; nichrome resists about 65 times more, so it is used in heaters and toasters. A thick copper wire has almost no resistance: connect it straight to a battery and a huge current flows \u2014 a short circuit, which is why fuses exist."},nonOhmic:{title:"When Ohm's law does not hold",text:"[[Ohm's law is not a law of nature for everything \u2014 only for some conductors, which are called ohmic.]] The filament of a light bulb is not: as it heats up to about 2700 \xB0C its resistance grows more than ten times, so the I\u2013U graph bends. A diode is even stranger: it passes the current in one direction only, and an LED starts to conduct only above a threshold voltage (about 1.9 V for a red one). For these, R = U/I still tells the resistance at a given moment, but it changes with the voltage \u2014 look at the graph of resistance."},power:{title:"Power and heat",text:"A current flowing through a resistance turns electric energy into heat at the rate P = U\xB7I, which with Ohm's law is also P = I\xB2\xB7R = U\xB2/R. That is how a heater, a kettle and a bulb filament work. It is also why wires get warm and why a short circuit is dangerous: a huge current makes a lot of heat (P = I\xB2R). Doubling the current makes four times the heat."},meters:{title:"The ammeter and the voltmeter",text:"An ammeter measures the current, so it is connected in series: the whole current passes through it, and it must have almost no resistance. A voltmeter measures the voltage across a component, so it is connected in parallel with it and must draw almost no current. In the picture the ammeter (A) is in the loop and the voltmeter (V) is wired across the component. A battery has a small internal resistance r too, so the voltage on the component is a little less than the battery's EMF \u03B5: U = \u03B5 \u2212 I\xB7r."},check:{title:"Check your understanding",text:"A 12 V battery drives a current through a 24 \u03A9 resistor. What is the current? (0.5 A.) The resistance is halved. What happens to the current? (It doubles.) A wire is made twice as long. What happens to its resistance? (It doubles.) The same wire is replaced by one twice as thick. (Its resistance becomes a quarter.) Why is the I\u2013U graph of a bulb a curve? (Its resistance grows as it heats up.) Test each answer in the simulation."}},formula:{ohm:"Ohm's law: the current in a resistor",resistance:"The resistance, from measured voltage and current",voltage:"The voltage across a resistor",wire:"The resistance of a wire: resistivity \xD7 length / cross-section",power:"The power turned into heat",battery:"A battery with internal resistance r: the current and the voltage on the component",diode:"Shockley's law for a diode (I_s: saturation current, V_T: thermal voltage)"},variable:{U:"Voltage across the component",I:"Current",R:"Resistance",P:"Power",L:"Length of the wire",rho:"Resistivity of the material (10\u207B\u2078 \u03A9\xB7m)"},assumption:{ideal:"The connecting wires have no resistance; the ammeter has none either, and the voltmeter draws no current, so neither disturbs the circuit.",steady:"The bulb and the wire are in a steady state at every moment (they heat up instantly); a wire's resistance does not change when it warms, and no wire melts, however big the current.",wire:"The picture of the wire is not to scale: a longer, thinner wire looks longer and thinner, but only roughly.",flow:"The moving dots show the direction and relative size of the current (a huge current is not drawn proportionally faster). Real charges drift far more slowly."},param:{component:"Component",mode:"Battery",voltage:"Battery voltage",sweepMax:"Largest voltage of the sweep",resistance:"Resistance",material:"Material of the wire",length:"Length of the wire",diameter:"Diameter of the wire",sweepPeriod:"Time of one full sweep",internal:"Internal resistance of the battery",timeStep:"Time step"},hint:{component:"A resistor, a wire, a light bulb or an LED. Compare their I\u2013U graphs.",mode:"It can rise and fall by itself, even reversing, or stay where you put it.",voltage:"Negative reverses the battery.",sweepMax:"The voltage goes from 0 up to this value, back through 0 to its negative, and home again.",resistance:"A bigger resistance lets less current through.",material:"Copper conducts best; nichrome is used in heaters.",length:"A longer wire resists more.",diameter:"A thicker wire resists less: the resistance goes as 1/d\xB2.",sweepPeriod:"How long one up-and-down sweep takes.",internal:"Real batteries lose part of their voltage inside when a current flows."},component:{resistor:"Resistor",wire:"Wire of a given material",bulb:"Light bulb",diode:"LED"},material:{copper:"Copper",aluminium:"Aluminium",iron:"Iron",constantan:"Constantan",nichrome:"Nichrome"},preset:{sweep:"Rises and falls by itself",fixed:"Stays where I put it",aa:"AA battery",flat:"Flat battery",nineVolt:"9 V battery",car:"Car battery",r5:"5 \u03A9",r10:"10 \u03A9",r47:"47 \u03A9",r100:"100 \u03A9"},measure:{voltage:"Voltage across the component",current:"Current",resistance:"Resistance U / I (shown up to 10 000 \u03A9)",power:"Power (heat per second)",emf:"EMF of the battery",slope:"Resistance for small changes \u0394U / \u0394I",lost:"Power lost inside the battery",temperature:"Temperature of the filament",wireResistance:"Resistance of the wire",resistivity:"Resistivity of the material (10\u207B\u2078 \u03A9\xB7m)",diodeVoltage:"Voltage across the diode itself"},view:{resistor:"Resistor {r}",wire:"{material} wire: {length} long, diameter {diameter} \xB7 R = {r}",bulb:"Light bulb: the filament is at {temperature}",diode:"LED with a {r} resistor in series",ohm:"I = U / R = {u} / {r} = {i}",diodeForward:"The LED conducts: U = {u}, I = {i}",diodeBlocked:"The LED blocks the current: U = {u}, I \u2248 0",short:"Short circuit! A huge current flows",legend:"Dots: the current, from the + terminal round the circuit",flowOption:"Moving charges"},graph:{iv:"Current against voltage (the I\u2013U graph)",resistance:"Resistance against voltage",power:"Power against voltage"},challenge:{oneAmp:{title:"One ampere",description:"Choose a resistor and hold the voltage so that exactly 1 A flows (give or take 0.02 A).",hint:"I = U / R: for example 10 V on 10 \u03A9."},quarterAmp:{title:"A quarter of an ampere at 10 volts",description:"Make the voltage 10 V and the current 0.25 A, with a resistor.",hint:"R = U / I = 10 / 0.25 = 40 \u03A9."},fiveWatts:{title:"Five watts of heat",description:"Make the resistor turn 5 W into heat (give or take 0.2 W).",hint:"P = U\xB2/R: for example 10 V on 20 \u03A9."},hotFilament:{title:"A hot filament",description:"Light the bulb so that its filament resistance is at least 10 \u03A9, ten times its cold value.",hint:"Choose the light bulb, hold the voltage and raise it to 5.5 V or more."},oneWay:{title:"The LED blocks",description:"Reverse the battery so that the LED lets no current through at \u22122 V or less.",hint:"Choose the LED and a negative voltage."},wire50:{title:"A 50-ohm wire",description:"Make a wire with a resistance of 50 \u03A9 (give or take 0.5 \u03A9).",hint:"A thin nichrome wire: R = \u03C1\xB7L/A. For example 2.25 m of 0.25 mm wire gives about 50 \u03A9."},shortCircuit:{title:"A short circuit",description:"Choose a wire that makes a current of 100 A or more flow.",hint:"A short, thick wire of copper has almost no resistance."}}}},a={sim:r};export{a as default,r as sim};
